---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.endsWith("/") ? import.meta.env.BASE_URL : import.meta.env.BASE_URL + "/";
---
<Layout title="Adopting Artificial Intelligence in Contemporary Society — Rahul Jain">
  <div class="wrap">
    <a class="back-link" href={base + "origins/"}>&larr; Origins</a>
    <h1 class="page-title">Adopting Artificial Intelligence in Contemporary Society</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">AP Seminar Performance Task, high school research paper</p>
    <section>
      <p class="blurb">
        Long before Hexxed or the Allen Institute, this was my first attempt at writing seriously about
        AI — weighing the economic and social case for adoption against the privacy, cost, and labor
        concerns it raises. Looking back, it's a rougher version of the same question I still ask: not
        whether a technology is powerful, but what it actually does to the people using it.
      </p>

      <div class="abstract-quote">
        <span class="abstract-label">Full Abstract</span>
        <p>
          Artificial Intelligence (AI) is a recent development in computing technology that allows
          machines to collect data and perform tasks that normally require human intelligence. One subset
          of AI is wearable technology, which collects data about users and their environments and then
          assists users in making intelligent decisions. A common contention within this arena of
          developing technology centers around whether such innovations should be suppressed in favor of
          natural biological evolution, or encouraged as part of an emerging world where machines help
          humans make informed decisions. How this dilemma is managed will shape contemporary society
          significantly, and is at the heart of this paper's investigation.
        </p>
        <p>
          While biological evolution and artificial intelligence developments are both beneficial,
          artificial intelligence provides additional economic, social, and medical benefits over natural
          evolution alone. Adoption of AI technologies can assist people with disabilities and help
          diagnose and prevent disease. The positive economic effects of AI adoption include better energy
          conservation practices and higher worker productivity resulting in higher profits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Beyond the benefits, however, real limitations to AI implementation exist, driven by the
          inherent fear of AI's pervasive powers extending far beyond the biological component. AI
          adoption is frequently framed as an ongoing threat to society, especially around <strong>privacy,
          expense, and job loss</strong> for non-skilled workers. Since successful AI implementation requires
          collecting user data, privacy concerns arise around companies that could have that data stolen.
          Both developing and implementing the technology is expensive, a cost likely passed on in price,
          which can lower adoption rates and lead to greater inefficiency as ubiquitous implementation is
          required for meaningful impact. Greater than the financial cost, however, is the cost to jobs:
          as machines are programmed for human tasks, non-skilled labor risks being replaced by fixed
          capital investment, leading to concerns about mass unemployment — a concern this paper argues is
          likely disproven in the long run, as <strong>new technologies tend to create more jobs than they
          eliminate</strong>.
        </p>
        <p>
          While AI development raises important considerations around privacy, cost, and potential
          unemployment, these can be addressed through thoughtful policy. The economic, social, and
          medical benefits strongly suggest continued investment in emerging AI technologies to decrease
          energy expenditures, increase worker productivity, help people with disabilities become equal
          participants in the workforce, and improve human well-being through preventive medicine. The
          paper recommends that government begin working with companies to <strong>prioritize R&amp;D investment
          in AI</strong>, develop policies ensuring <strong>skills training for workers</strong> in advanced technologies, and
          increase societal awareness to improve national health through wearable technology and devices.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Layout>
